{"type": "bubble", "x": 200, "y": 500},
{"type": "air_cushion", "x": 100, "y": 300, "angle": 45}
]
}

7.2 Scoring Algorithm
The original Cut the Rope scoring system is a hybrid of Star Collection (binary requirements for unlocking levels) and a numeric Score (for leaderboards).
Star Logic:
Stars are boolean flags (Collected/Not Collected).
Unlocking the next "Box" (world) requires a summation of stars: $\sum Stars \ge \text{Threshold}$.21
Numeric Score Formula:
The score is calculated upon level completion:

$$\text{Score} = (N_{stars} \times V_{star}) + (T_{remaining} \times V_{time})$$
$N_{stars}$: Number of stars collected (0-3).
$V_{star}$: Value per star (typically 1000 or 2000 points).
$T_{remaining}$: Time left on the invisible level timer.
$V_{time}$: Points per second (e.g., 10 points/sec). This encourages not just solving the puzzle, but solving it fast.22
Timed Stars:
Some levels feature stars with a shrinking circle.
Logic: A timer star.timer decrements by dt every frame.
If star.timer <= 0, the star entity is removed from the space and cannot be collected.
Research suggests the duration varies per level, often between 3-10 seconds depending on the puzzle difficulty.23 8. Development Roadmap and Milestones
To successfully replicate the game, a phased approach is recommended.
Phase
Milestone
Technical Focus
1
The Pendulum
Set up Pymunk space. Create a single rope chain with a weight. Tune PinJoint parameters and solver iterations until it swings without jitter.
2
The Cut
Implement mouse tracking and segment_query. Successfully break constraints and observe realistic falling physics.
3
The Goal
Add Om Nom and Candy collision logic. Implement the "Win State" trigger.
4
The Toys
Implement Bubbles (buoyancy) and Air Cushions (impulses). These are the fundamental puzzle elements.
5
The Polish
Replace debug lines with Sprite rendering. Implement Catmull-Rom splines for the rope. Add sound effects.
6
The Content
Build the Level Loader (JSON parser) and design 5-10 test levels.

9. Conclusion
   Replicating Cut the Rope is a demanding exercise in simulation programming. The "magic" of the game lies not in complex AI or graphics, but in the nuanced tuning of a rigid body physics engine. The distinction between a mediocre clone and a faithful replica rests on three pillars:
   Stability: Using high solver iterations (30+) and symplectic integration to ensure ropes feel solid, not rubbery.
   Interaction: Precise raycasting algorithms for cutting that feel responsive to user input.
   Completeness: Implementing the full suite of toys—bubbles, spiders, magic hats—with their specific kinematic and dynamic rules.
   By following the architectural patterns outlined in this report—specifically the use of Pymunk's PinJoint chains, collision bitmasks for filtering, and impulse-based control for gadgets—a developer can reconstruct the mechanical heart of this classic game with high fidelity.
